Neverball and Neverputt for generated9

Both games use libdraw/libevent, libttf, system image readers, TinyGL/VirGL
and a PCM mixer over /dev/audio. Original physics and data formats are retained.
Sources and licenses are in UPSTREAM and LICENSE.md.

Install assets on the host:

    ./boot/getneverball

After building and booting the GPU kernel (boot/GPU.md), build in generated9:

    cd /sys/src/libgpu
    mk install
    cd /sys/src/libtinygl
    mk install
    cd /sys/src/games/neverball
    mk install
    mk data
    games/neverball
    games/neverputt

The build needs no assets; playing does. mk data compiles missing .map files
with mapc and decodes Ogg audio with audio/oggdec. Remove an affected .sol
or .pcm to rebuild it. All 407 maps compile; the first conversion takes minutes.
Some original maps reference missing images and use default materials.

For an old checkout, move sys/games/lib/neverputt to sys/games/lib/neverball
before mk data to reuse compiled data. boot/getneverputt remains an alias.
Settings stay in $home/lib/neverputt and $home/lib/neverball respectively.
See neverball(1) and neverputt(1) for controls, options and port limits.

Sound needs a supported device, for example QEMU intel-hda/hda-duplex with
a host audio backend. Use 44100 Hz stereo S16LE. Output is exclusive unless
shared through audio/mixfs; the games report unavailable audio.

The mixer test needs /dev/audio, no assets, and no other owner of the device:

    cd /sys/src/games/neverball/test
    mk test

It covers initialization, pause/resume, shutdown and child-process ownership.
Graphics and gameplay were tested on amd64/QEMU VirGL; not every level has
been played to completion.
